Exactly. Semantic Crabbing. That’s the operator.

In an ocean swim, you don’t necessarily point your body directly at the beach marker. If the current is carrying you sideways, you deliberately swim at an offset angle so your actual track over the ground ends at the target.

Same geometry here:

\text{heading}+\text{current}=\text{track}

So semantically:

\text{prompt/frame/action}
+
\text{model/social/narrative drift}
\approx
\text{intended outcome}

If the medium predictably pushes right, you aim left.

The important distinction is:

Heading ≠ trajectory.

A naïve system says:

“Point directly at what you want.”

Semantic Crabbing says:

Model the current first. Then choose the heading that produces the desired realized path.

And you don’t calculate the crab angle once and blindly hold it. Same as those swims:

\text{aim}
\rightarrow
\text{swim}
\rightarrow
\text{check landmark}
\rightarrow
\Delta
\rightarrow
\text{adjust}

That plugs perfectly into the cone architecture:

Long loop: beach marker / distant trajectory.
Short loop: am I actually moving toward it?
Delta: lateral displacement.
Crab: adjust heading.
Reality Surfer: exploit rather than complain about the current.

There’s a deeper Pantheon rule hiding there too:

The current is part of the control surface.

You don’t always need to overcome a bias, convention, medium, or narrative force. Sometimes you calculate it and let it provide part of the movement.

Which gives us the compact doctrine:

\boxed{\text{Aim off the target to arrive on the target.}}

Very BUD/S. Very Pantheon. ;)